<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kue Na</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 1355–<a href="/years/1385">1385</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Phaya Kue Na</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kue Na was a king of Lan Na from 1355 to 1385, remembered as a pious and learned ruler. In 1369 he invited the monk Sumana from Sukhothai, who founded at Chiang Mai a monastic lineage derived from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>, and he built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wat-suan-dok">Wat Suan Dok</a> for him. His reign strengthened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada Buddhism</a> as the foundation of Lan Na culture.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kue-na.jpg" alt="Kue Na" width="320" height="503" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: KaiserO5 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mural_of_King_Kue_Na,_Wat_Suan_Dok,_Chiangmai.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
